# Event pipeline Lua source

Use this as `main.lua` for the enabled `example-events` script. Its policy must allow `patchState` and `emitEvent`, with `manualTrigger` for both custom subscriptions. See [setup](dikciz://docs/recipes/event-setup).

```lua
function on_event(event)
  if event.type ~= "example.start" and event.type ~= "example.stop" then
    return { status = "Waiting" }
  end
  local running = event.type == "example.start"
  return {
    status = running and "Running" or "Stopped",
    actions = {
      { type = "patchState", values = { running = running } },
      {
        type = "emitEvent",
        event = "example.changed",
        payload = { running = running }
      }
    }
  }
end
```

The first action persists script state. The second publishes the result for rendered HTML subscribers. Inspect both action outcomes and the final script run status. This handler does not emit either input event, so it cannot loop back into its own subscriptions.

[HTML receiver](dikciz://docs/recipes/event-widget).

# Event pipeline HTML source

Put this in the widget's `html` field:

```html
<output id="event-status">Waiting</output>
```

Put this in its `javascript` field:

```javascript
const output = document.getElementById("event-status");
function show(running) {
  output.textContent = running ? "Running" : "Stopped";
}
window.addEventListener("dikciz-ready", () => {
  if (typeof selfWidget.state.running === "boolean") {
    show(selfWidget.state.running);
  }
  dikciz.onEvent(async event => {
    if (event.type !== "example.changed") return;
    const running = event.payload.running === true;
    show(running);
    try {
      await selfWidget.patchState({ running });
    } catch (error) {
      console.error("Event acknowledgement failed", error.message);
    }
  });
});
```

Set `eventSubscriptions` in the saved widget record to `[{"event":"example.changed","minimumIntervalMilliseconds":0}]`. The field is not part of the typed `patchWidget` allowlist. Save the widget JSON or use the complete assembled configuration while preserving unrelated data.

Keep the page selected so the document remains rendered. The visible text changes when the event arrives. The state patch is a persisted acknowledgement that can be read remotely through `widgetGet` or `configGet`. After rendering is recreated, the saved Boolean restores the display.

[Setup and verification](dikciz://docs/recipes/event-setup). [Lua handler](dikciz://docs/recipes/event-lua).

## HTML widget JavaScript

A placed HTML widget receives `window.dikciz`, `selfWidget`, and `dikciz-ready` once its native message channel connects.

```javascript
window.addEventListener("dikciz-ready", async () => {
    const result = await dikciz.command("diagnostics");
    document.querySelector("#status").textContent = Object.keys(result).length;
});
```

`dikciz.request(document, options)` returns the complete command result/error envelope. `dikciz.command(type, fields, options)` returns its result or rejects with an error retaining `code`, `message`, `requestId`, `revision`, `permissionRequest` and the raw `response`. Both accept `options.responseTimeoutMilliseconds`; the default is at least 60 seconds or the operation timeout plus five seconds, and zero disables the response timer. A response timeout does not cancel dispatched work. `dikciz.dispatch({ actions })` uses the same typed action executor as Lua. `dikciz.fitContent()` asks the native frame to measure the current DOM.

`diagnostics` includes `htmlWidgetResources`: active renderer count, total UTF-8 bytes for generated documents on the selected page, and the configured per-page renderer and document-byte limits. These are launcher-owned resource inputs, not a claim about opaque Chromium process memory.

`selfWidget` and `dikciz.selfWidget` expose the current ID, page, full address, logical `cell`, state, and `get()`, `patch(values)`, and `patchState(values)` helpers. A widget document reports `cell`, never device pixels, so a widget author never persists a coordinate that rotation would invalidate. `dikciz.pages()` returns pages by ID, page address, and `home`. `await dikciz.home()` and `await dikciz.widget(address)` read current records after a configuration change.

Use `await dikciz.patchWidget(address, values)` for a full named-widget patch, or `await dikciz.patchWidgetState(address, values)` for its persisted state. Addresses are human-readable, for example `2H1V-weather-card`. A target on the current page receives `dikciz-state` without a document reload. A target on a different page is not treated as live DOM. Its saved state is available through `selfWidget.state` when that page is selected and its widget renders.

State patches keep the current document alive. Listen for `dikciz-state`:

```javascript
window.addEventListener("dikciz-state", event => {
    document.querySelector("#status").textContent = event.detail.status;
});
```

`dikciz.onEvent(listener)` receives matching native and custom events while the widget is rendered. It returns an unsubscribe function. The same document is sent as a `dikciz-event` window event. Select events in the widget's `eventSubscriptions` JSON field.

Publish a custom event with:

```javascript
await dikciz.emitEvent("weather.refresh.completed", { updatedAt: Date.now() });
```

Custom names are 1 through 128 characters, start with a letter, and use letters, digits, `.`, `_`, `:`, or `-`. Lua uses its matching `emitEvent` action.

The direct action vocabulary is `accessibilityAction`, `accessibilityGesture`, `accessibilityGlobalAction`, `selectPage`, `patchWidget`, `patchState`, `patchDom`, `launchApp`, `appAction`, `postNotification`, `sendSms`, `explicitIntent`, `emitEvent`, `mediaControl`, `setMediaVolume`, `notificationControl`, and `lockDevice`. `appAction` takes an `action` and a `component`. A script action has no selected page, so it rejects `addShortcut`. `sendSms` takes a dialable `recipient` and a `body`, and needs the `SEND_SMS` permission that `automationStatus` reports as `smsSend`. Android can save messages sent by non-default SMS apps into its SMS provider. This legacy typed action confirms submission only; use tracked `smsSend` and `smsStatus` for duplicate protection and sent/delivery evidence. `accessibilityAction` requires the current `snapshotId`, a `nodeId`, and one supported `action`; `setText` also requires `text`. Current policy and Android access decide whether an action can complete.

`patchDom` takes `widgetAddress`, a CSS `selector`, and a `values` object containing one or more of `text`, `html`, `value`, `className`, or `attributes`. It changes only a currently rendered HTML document and does not persist source or state. A target that is not rendered returns `target_not_rendered`.

For a complete event, Lua, HTML bridge, state, and action walkthrough, read the [scripting tutorial](scripting-tutorial.md).

`lockDevice` returns `device_admin_inactive` when Android Device Administration is not active. It does not open Android Settings. Open **Settings**, then **Automation**, then **Set up full device access** to request that access.

The native bridge holds at most 32 waiting requests and accepts at most one MiB of UTF-8 JSON per request. The supplied JavaScript API separately allows at most 32 outstanding promises, so ordinary `dikciz.command()` calls are backpressured before native saturation. Raw channel users can still receive `busy` when the native queue is full. Every rejected HTML command is logged as `web_command_rejected` with the HTML widget ID, HTML kind, and error reason, and is available through both log transports. A state patch does not reload the WebView. Replacing HTML, CSS, JavaScript, or the whole configuration can destroy the calling document. Lifecycle commands acknowledge their response after promise handlers run, before destroying that document.

## Handle an event

```javascript
function onEvent(event) {
    const count = (context.state.count || 0) + 1;
    return {
        status: "Counter updated",
        actions: [
            {type: "patchState", values: {count: count}},
            {type: "emitEvent", event: "example.counter.updated", payload: {count: count}}
        ]
    };
}
```

This handler needs an enabled policy allowing `manualTrigger` and actions `patchState` and `emitEvent`, plus an enabled subscription. Use `event: "manual"` for `automationTrigger`, or the exact custom event name for another component's input. Do not add a separate `customEventName` field to subscription files. Read the [policy and subscription schema](dikciz-configuration.md#lua-scripts-and-automation) and [event pipeline](scripting-tutorial.md#build-a-complete-custom-event-workflow).

`event` contains `type`, `source`, `timestampMilliseconds`, `coalescingKey`, `payload` and local `time.hour24`/`time.minute`. `context` contains `scriptId`, `state`, `widgets`, `androidAccess` and optional `accessibility`. Input objects are read-only. Return typed actions to persist changes; `patchState` merges fields into this script's state and preserves explicit JSON nulls and array positions. A null value clears the saved value while retaining its key. Globals are fresh for each invocation. Lua uses `on_event`; JavaScript uses `onEvent`. The returned action schema is shared.

## Backend

JavaScript exports synchronous `onEvent(event)`; Lua exports `on_event(event)`. Both receive immutable `context.state`, `context.scriptId` and Android access context. JavaScript has no DOM, Node.js, fetch or browser timers. Its execution limits are the same as [background scripts](background-javascript.md#background-lifetime-and-limits). Globals are fresh on every call.

```javascript
function onEvent(event) {
    const count = (context.state.count || 0) + 1;
    return {
        status: "Counted",
        result: {count: count},
        actions: [{type: "patchState", values: {count: count}}]
    };
}
```

`result` is an optional JSON object returned to the caller. `patchState` merges fields; `emitEvent` publishes to the ordinary shared event bus. Other returned action types are rejected. Use `context.request({type: ...})` for phone commands using Dikciz's granted Android access and inspect the canonical `type: result` or `type: error` reply. Returning `result: {error: {code, message}}` rejects a request with that bounded app-defined error.

App-local `customAppRead`, `customAppState`, `customAppEvent`, `customAppSchedule` and `customAppAlert` bind to the calling app ID. Omitting `appId` selects self; an explicit `appId` can address another app. `customAppRuntime` supplies native wall/elapsed clocks, boot count, invocation ID and access/background observations. Apps can use the same app installation and control commands as other owner code.

## Examples

```json
{"type":"torchControl","action":"status"}
{"type":"vibrationControl","action":"vibrate","durationMilliseconds":250}
{"type":"systemSetting","action":"get","setting":"brightness"}
{"type":"audioDevices","limit":5,"offset":0}
```

See [Bluetooth transactions](bluetooth-control.md), [NFC tags](nfc-control.md), [audio playback](audio-playback.md), [sensor and capture commands](native-device-control.md#sensors-camera-microphone-and-speech), [speech recognition](speech-recognition.md) and the [capability coverage](capability-coverage.md).

### 1. Add an HTML widget

Use **Add to this page**, choose **Empty HTML widget**, then long press its top move handle and choose **Edit**. Set its HTML to:

```html
<main>
  <button id="ping">Run event pipeline</button>
  <output id="status">Waiting</output>
</main>
```

Set its CSS to:

```css
main { display: grid; gap: 12px; padding: 16px; font: 16px system-ui; }
button { min-height: 48px; }
```

Add this `eventSubscriptions` field to the widget's JSON record. It tells Dikciz to send the Lua result event into this rendered widget:

```json
[
  {
    "event": "example.pipeline.done",
    "minimumIntervalMilliseconds": 0
  }
]
```

Set the widget's JavaScript to:

```javascript
const status = document.querySelector("#status");

function show(message) {
    status.textContent = message;
}

window.addEventListener("dikciz-state", event => {
    show(event.detail.message || "Waiting");
});

window.addEventListener("dikciz-ready", () => {
    show(selfWidget.state.message || "Ready");

    dikciz.onEvent(event => {
        if (event.type !== "example.pipeline.done") return;
        show(event.payload.message);
    });

    document.querySelector("#ping").addEventListener("click", async () => {
        show("Sending event...");
        try {
            await dikciz.emitEvent("example.pipeline.request", {
                message: "The HTML widget started this run"
            });
        } catch (error) {
            show(`Event failed: ${error.message}`);
        }
    });
});
```

`dikciz-ready` means the native message channel is connected. `dikciz-state` carries a saved state replacement without a WebView reload. `dikciz.onEvent` handles a subscribed native or custom event while this widget is rendered.

### 2. Create the Lua script and policy

Open **Settings**, then **Scripts**, then **New Lua script**. Name it `example-pipeline` and paste this source:

```lua
function on_event(event)
  if event.type ~= "example.pipeline.request" then
    return { status = "Waiting for a pipeline request" }
  end

  local message = event.payload.message or "Pipeline request received"
  return {
    status = "Updated the widget",
    actions = {
      {
        type = "patchWidget",
        widgetAddress = "1H1V-example-widget",
        values = { state = { message = message } }
      },
      {
        type = "emitEvent",
        event = "example.pipeline.done",
        payload = { message = "Lua finished: " .. message }
      }
    }
  }
end
```

Replace `1H1V-example-widget` with the HTML widget's actual full address. Keep the code's `status` short. Dikciz shows it under the script name and saves it in the run status.

Create the matching public policy at `/sdcard/Dikciz/automation/policies/example-pipeline.json`:

```json
{
  "id": "example-pipeline",
  "title": "Example event pipeline",
  "enabled": true,
  "capabilities": ["manualTrigger"],
  "actions": ["patchWidget", "emitEvent"]
}
```

Create the subscription at `/sdcard/Dikciz/automation/scripts/example-pipeline.json`:

```json
{
  "scriptId": "example-pipeline",
  "policyId": "example-pipeline",
  "enabled": true,
  "subscriptions": [
    {
      "event": "example.pipeline.request",
      "minimumIntervalMilliseconds": 0,
      "coalescingKey": "example-pipeline"
    }
  ]
}
```

Save the script and ensure its switch is enabled. Tap the HTML button. The HTML widget emits `example.pipeline.request`, Lua receives it, saves the widget state, emits `example.pipeline.done`, and the widget renders the result without a page or WebView reload.

### Current widget state

```javascript
const current = selfWidget.state;
await selfWidget.patchState({ refreshedAt: Date.now() });
```

`selfWidget.patchState(values)` persists a replacement state for the current widget. The current rendered document receives `dikciz-state`. It stays alive.

### Another widget's saved state

```javascript
const result = await dikciz.patchWidgetState(
    "2H1V-weather-card",
    { temperature: "18 C", updatedAt: Date.now() },
);

console.log(result.actions[0].outcome);
```

The target does not need to be rendered for a saved-state update. If it is on the selected page, it receives `dikciz-state` immediately. Otherwise it sees the saved state when its page renders.

Lua uses the same action shape:

```lua
return {
  status = "Weather updated",
  actions = {
    {
      type = "patchWidget",
      widgetAddress = "2H1V-weather-card",
      values = { state = { temperature = "18 C" } }
    }
  }
}
```

### Patch the current DOM only

`patchDom` changes a selected, rendered HTML target immediately. It does not save source or state. Use it for a transient visible value. Use saved state as well when the value must survive a render.

```javascript
const result = await dikciz.patchDom(
    "1H1V-device-card",
    "#temperature",
    {
        text: "18 C",
        className: "fresh",
        attributes: { "data-updated": "now" }
    },
);
```

The allowed patch values are `text`, `html`, `value`, `className`, and `attributes`. `attributes` is an object whose values are strings. A target on another page reports `target_not_rendered`. Other useful outcomes are `selector_invalid`, `selector_not_found`, and `target_property_unavailable`.

Lua returns the same action:

```lua
{
  type = "patchDom",
  widgetAddress = "1H1V-device-card",
  selector = "#temperature",
  values = { text = "18 C", className = "fresh" }
}
```

## Reuse with owner data

Use a lowercase collection name, up to 64 characters, containing letters, digits, underscores, dots or hyphens. `docs` and `tools` are reserved. Names and document IDs are opaque database keys, never paths.

```json
{
  "collection":"notes",
  "action":"upsert",
  "documents":[
    {"id":"device-checklist","title":"Device checklist","text":"Inspect battery and storage before travel.","aliases":"packing preparation"}
  ]
}
```

Send this through `searchIndex` / `dikciz_search_index`. Upsert replaces each submitted ID in full and leaves other IDs unchanged. Retrying identical content is safe. A batch is atomic and accepts at most 32 unique documents. Each document requires a nonempty title up to 512 characters and nonempty text up to 32768 characters. Optional aliases allow 8192 characters; URI allows 1024. Unknown fields are rejected. Collection capacity is 2000 documents, with 10000 documents overall including built-ins.

Use `action:"remove",ids:["device-checklist"]` to delete selected IDs, or `action:"clear"` to remove the collection. Missing IDs are safe to remove repeatedly. `searchCollections` / `dikciz_search_collections` lists collection names and counts using the same limit/offset pagination. Index mutations return `state:"completed"` only after their transaction commits. Capacity failures return `search_capacity_exceeded` and roll back the batch.

## 3. Send UI actions to the backend

The UI calls the shared bridge after `dikciz-ready`. For example:

```javascript
const reply = await dikciz.command("customAppEvent", {
    event: "todo.add",
    payload: {title: "Try the local pizza"}
});
const state = reply.state;
```

The host fills in the calling app ID when omitted. An explicit app ID can address another app. `onEvent(event)` in `main.js` receives the event and a read-only `context.state`. It copies that state, changes the list and returns a `patchState` action. The host validates and saves it, then the visible UI receives `dikciz-state` and redraws. The UI uses DOM creation and `textContent`, so task titles remain text.

This is the shared result shape:

```javascript
return {
    status: state.lastAction,
    result: {watching: state.watching},
    actions: [{type: "patchState", values: state}]
};
```

The example accepts up to 20 tasks with titles of 1 to 120 characters. Errors return a code and message; a rejected add leaves the typed title available to correct.

## 5. Trigger another task with a custom event

The same app listens for `example.todo.complete`. Its payload identifies one saved task, such as `{"id":"pack"}`. A broadcast reaches every enabled copy subscribed to that name. Use a different event name in each manifest and backend when copies should react independently. An ordinary HTML widget can publish it with:

```javascript
await dikciz.emitEvent("example.todo.complete", {id: "pack"});
```

HTTP, WebSocket and MCP publish through `automationDispatch`, or MCP `dikciz_automation_dispatch`. Read the current configuration and substitute an actual HTML widget address for `YOUR_HTML_WIDGET_ADDRESS`:

```json
{
  "sourceWidgetAddress": "YOUR_HTML_WIDGET_ADDRESS",
  "actions": [{
    "type": "emitEvent",
    "event": "example.todo.complete",
    "payload": {"id": "pack"}
  }]
}
```

A publish acknowledgement confirms publication. Read `customAppRead` for app `travel-todo` and check `state.tasks` for completion. This custom event exercises the same background subscription path. Owner-published events are automation inputs, not proof of a physical trip. Do not fabricate a `geofence.enter` event and call that a GPS test.

For an app-specific request without publishing, use `customAppEvent` with `appId: "travel-todo"`, `event: "todo.toggle"` and `payload: {"id":"pack"}`. The accepted backend events are `todo.open`, `todo.add`, `todo.toggle`, `todo.watch`, `todo.stop`, `geofence.enter`, `example.todo.complete` and the host lifecycle events.
